Kheda and the nearby Balasinor area are among the richest dinosaur sites in India. Thousands of fossil eggs have been found there, many still in nests. A fossil park at Raiyoli, near Balasinor, now protects part of the site.

The same rocks hold a surprise. In 2010, scientists described [[@lost-titanoboa|a fossil snake]] called Sanajeh, found in Gujarat coiled around a crushed dinosaur egg beside a hatchling. Rajasaurus shared its world with egg thieves.

{{story:india-drift}}

Hail needs a [[@thunder-sound|thunderstorm]], the kind of storm that also brings [[@lightning|lightning]], with powerful winds blowing upwards inside it, called updrafts. A small ice pellet is carried high into the freezing top of the cloud, where supercooled droplets freeze onto it. It falls, gets swept up again, and adds another layer. The stronger the updraft, the bigger the [[@hailstorms-india|hailstone]] can grow before it falls.

That's why hail is most common in warm [[@seasons-why|seasons]], when thunderstorms are strongest. In India, hailstorms often strike in March, April and May, damaging crops of wheat, mangoes and vegetables. In the USA, the central plains, from Texas up to South Dakota, get some of the most frequent hail in the world.

{{story:snowflake-six}}

When a new asteroid is found, astronomers know its path only roughly at first. They calculate the chance that it [[@space-asteroid-defence|might hit Earth]], using [[@probability|probability]]. As more observations come in, the path becomes clearer, and almost always the chance drops to zero.

{{story:probability}}

That is what happened with an asteroid called 2024 YR4, found in December 2024. For a few weeks in early 2025, its chance of hitting Earth in 2032 rose to about 3 per cent, one of the highest ever recorded for an asteroid that size. Then new measurements ruled out an Earth impact completely. This is how the system is meant to work.

On islands with no cats, dogs or snakes, a bird that feeds on the ground has little reason to fly away. Over time, many island birds lost the ability. The [[@dodo|dodo]] of Mauritius, a giant pigeon, was one.

[[@new-zealand|New Zealand]] had almost no land [[mammal|mammals]] before people arrived, so birds took their place. The kakapo is a heavy, green, flightless parrot that climbs trees and waddles at night. It nearly vanished after people brought [[@invasive-species|rats, cats and stoats]]. Thanks to careful protection, and a record breeding season, about 325 kakapo were alive in 2026.

{{story:dodo}}

The Greek scientist Archimedes, who lived in Syracuse in Sicily about 2,250 years ago, was the first known person to prove the rule of the lever with mathematics. He was so confident that, according to later writers, he said that with a place to stand and a long enough lever, he could move the Earth.

He was right in principle. The lever would have to be unimaginably long, and he would need to push it a vast distance to shift the planet even a tiny bit.

{{story:pyramids}}

Many inventors made glowing bulbs. In 1879, Thomas Edison's team in the USA made one that lasted long enough to be useful, and Joseph Swan in England made one too. Edison also built the power stations and wires needed to light whole streets.

Edison's team tested thousands of materials for the filament. One of the best was carbonised [[@bamboo|bamboo]], from Japan, which could glow for over a thousand hours. Later bulbs used tungsten, a metal that can survive enormous heat.

{{story:fireflies}}

His first famous phase is called the Blue Period, from about 1901 to 1904. After a close friend died, Picasso painted poor and lonely people almost entirely in shades of blue. The pictures feel cold and sad.

Then came the Rose Period, with warmer pinks and oranges and pictures of [[@circus|circus]] performers, acrobats and clowns. Both periods still look fairly realistic. What came next did not.

{{story:shapes-angles}}

Brahmagupta, working in Rajasthan, wrote his great book in 628 CE. He called positive numbers dhana, meaning fortunes, and negative numbers rina, meaning debts. Thinking about money made the rules easy to picture. If you owe a friend 10 rupees and pay back 4, you still owe 6, so −10 + 4 = −6.

A debt taken from zero becomes a fortune. A fortune times a debt is a debt. And a debt times a debt is a fortune, which is why two minuses multiplied make a plus. Brahmagupta also wrote rules for [[@zero-invent|zero]], treating it as a number of its own.

{{story:money-banks}}

In 1973, psychologists at Stanford University in California watched nursery children who loved drawing with markers. They split them into groups. One group was promised a "Good Player" certificate for drawing. Another drew with no reward. A third got a surprise certificate afterwards.

A week or two later, the markers were put out during free play. The children who had expected a certificate spent less time drawing than the others. The researchers argued the reward had changed how the children saw drawing: from play to work. Psychologists still argue about how strong this effect is. Rewards for boring jobs seem to do little harm, and praise for effort can help. Still, the study changed how many schools think about prizes.

{{story:video-games}}

In [[@mumbai|Mumbai]], the [[@india-wonder-mumbai-local|suburban trains]] carry several million passengers a day. At rush hour, trains built for about 1,800 people can carry far more. People hang from doors, play cards on the way to work and know exactly which carriage stops nearest the station exit.

The same trains help the city's dabbawalas carry lunches across town, sorting boxes at stations and riding the trains between them. Ladies' compartments, first-class carriages and luggage vans all have their own regulars. For many Mumbaikars, a seat by the window on a slow train is a small daily luxury.

{{story:lunchbox-dabbawala}}

The Scratch website is also a huge online community. Tens of millions of young people around the world have shared projects there. Anyone can look inside a project to see how it works, and "remix" it to make their own version, with credit to the original maker.

Scratch is available in dozens of languages, including Hindi, Tamil, Bengali and Spanish, so children can code in the language they speak at home. A simpler version, ScratchJr, made for children aged about 5 to 7, runs on tablets.

